time now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week according  to the American Cancer Society lung  cancer accounts for about fourteen  percent of all new cancer cases a new  study out this week published in the  Journal of Clinical Oncology examined  how certain types of vitamins might  raise your risk a major finding was that  men who have smoked cigarettes and taken  b6 and b12 supplements for 10 years or  more have a thirty to forty percent  higher risk of lung cancer those taking  the highest doses of the vitamins had  about double the risk for current  smokers the risk was even higher no  effect was seen among women here with  more is CBS News chief medical  correspondent dr. Jon LaPook and CBS  News contributor dr. Tara Narula Tara  give us some of the main findings of  this study for both the smokers and  non-smokers I think it's really  important that we're talking about this  because if you see the headline vitamins  causes cancer it can be very misleading  this was a specific population of  individuals essentially either former  current or recent smokers it was an  observational study which means it  doesn't prove cause and effect and it  essentially looked at the use of b6 b12  b9 and multi vitamins over a long period  of time in this population and they  didn't indeed find an association the  association was strongest in current  smokers who took very high doses of b6  and b12 over a 10-year period in that  group there was a three to four times  increased risk of the development of  lung cancer now they did not see this in  women they did not see it in the  multivitamins they did not see it in  folate the thinking is that it's  possible that because between these B  vitamins are involved in DNA cell growth  repair and maintenance that somehow by  tinkering with the amounts in a  population of smokers with male hormones  over a long period of time at these  higher doses you might either stimulate  the development of new cancers or  accelerate the development of cancers  that are already there John did the  authors give any idea why the vitamins  might have this kind of impact in men  versus women there's some speculation  all right there are differences in the  way enzymes work between between men and  women and these are important functions  in the way  that DNA is synthesized and repaired  there are hormonal differences as Tara  alluded to and then also there may be  differences between men and women in the  very susceptibility to lung cancer in  the first place  Tara it's no surprise a group  representing the supplement industry  says that these findings conflict with  previous research quote the numerous  benefits of V vitamins from food and  dietary supplements including supporting  cognition heart health and energy levels  are well established so what do we know  about the health benefits of vitamin B  so what we know is that for most  individuals in this country there really  is no good data to support the use of  supplements unless you are deficient now  there are some populations that are  deficient in B vitamins people who may  have celiac Crohn's disease or other  disorders there are strict vegetarians  in that population there may be benefit  but in general the bulk of the research  does not show any preventive benefits in  terms of for instance cancer and so  there may be this signal of harm we  don't know but certainly when you're  looking at the doses we're talking about  the recommended daily allowance for  example that you get in a regular  multivitamin is about one point three to  one point five milligrams of b6 about  two point four micrograms of b12 in this  study we were talking about greater than  twenty milligrams of these six greater  than 55 micrograms of b12 that's about  ten to twenty times the amount you're  gonna get in a regular multivitamin so  again very large doses note our next  topic deals with the potential link  between sleep and dementia a new study  in the journal Neurology looked at how  the two may relate the research looked  at 321 participants who were over the  age of 60 at the start they were  followed for an average of 12 years  there were 32 dementia cases observed  with 24 due to Alzheimer's disease Jon  did the study what did the studies show  exactly in this relationship between  sleep and dementia well we know with  sleep there are the different parts of  the cycle and it starts with kind of a  light sleep it goes to heavy sleep and  then you get REM rapid eye movement  that's where we dream and they found  that as the amount of REM went down the  risk of dementia went up that's scary  news for a lot of people and I think  we've all heard of REM but we don't  necessarily know what happens in the  different sleep cycles why is that one  so important yeah so for a long time the  thinking was that sleep is this very  passive inactive state where your body's  essentially  shutting down and then with the  development of the EEG in the 1930s we  realized that actually during periods of  time particularly during REM your brain  can actually be more awake than like  when you're awake and so when they  looked at the brainwaves they saw that  during REM sleep you essentially have  kind of small fast brainwaves as opposed  to non REM sleep where you have large  kind of slow brainwaves REM sleep is  characterized by vivid dreams as John  mentioned your muscles remain paralyzed  your breathing increases your heart rate  increases your blood pressure increases  as opposed to non REM sleep which again  is much more kind of quiet you cycle  through these stages throughout the  night about twenty to twenty-five  percent of your night is spent during  REM and we don't know why you cycle but  the theory one theory is that it may  help with memory consolidation which  could play into some of the issues of  dementia John is this study saying that  there is a definitive link between sleep  quality and dementia we don't know that  yet and we don't know if it's cause and  effect even if there is a link and one  interesting theory is that there's kind  of a garbage collecting function during  sleep that you have these toxins that  accumulate and then at night it sort of  gets cleaned out but people shouldn't  freak out I know there have been a lot  of discussion about that we're all kind  of sleep deprived of sleep even if it is  a cause and effect it would only be one  factor there are a lot of other factors  including just living a healthy  lifestyle so but it is an interesting  and compelling beginning of research  finally inspired by a heavenly site  people came together here on earth  earlier this week Americans  coast-to-coast got to see at least some  of the great American Eclipse a thrill  for folks of all ages and something that  hasn't happened nationwide for almost a  hundred years  dr. LaPook got the chance to experience  the Eclipse at totality on a visit to  Oregon with his family John how does  this event impact empathy and I mean I  guess could more events have a similar  effect well I think what was not  captured by all the pictures was the  spirituality of the event and my son  Noah called it collective wonder I mean  we know we live in a world that's very  troubled then there are a lot of  differences among us but we also live in  a world that's miraculous after this one  moment we were all looking up at the sky  at this spectacular event not a  difference among us and maybe one moment  could become two moments and maybe  collective wonder could become a more  important force in the universe  that's what I learned from my son Noah  during the Eclipse elective one great  phrase  you guys both had totality we didn't  here in New York but it was the same  thing seeing everybody on the rooftops  looking up it really was an amazing  moment  doctors Jon LaPook and Tara Narula thank  you very much it played a crucial role  in world war two but was destined not to  survive it up next we'll visit the just  discovered wreck of the USS Indianapolis  and hear from those who survived its  sinking an ordeal that even inspired a  character in the movie Jaws you're  watching CBS this morning Saturday
 

